      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mb-3">
        Quick Executive Summary: The 183-Day Rule &amp; Worldwide Taxation
      </h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        <li>
          <strong>The Quantitative Standard:</strong> An individual is classified as an Indonesian domestic tax resident (<em>Subjek Pajak Dalam Negeri / SPDN</em>) by being physically present in Indonesia for <strong>more than 183 days</strong> within any continuous 12-month timeframe.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The Rolling Window Trap:</strong> The 183-day counter does not reset on January 1st. It applies across any rolling 365-day period, meaning cross-year stays automatically count.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tax Scope:</strong> Domestic residents pay progressive rates (5% to 35%) on global worldwide income. Non-residents pay a flat 20% final withholding tax (PPh 26) strictly on Indonesian-sourced income.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Relief Mechanisms:</strong> Article 24 Foreign Tax Credits (PPh 24) and over 70 bilateral Double Taxation Avoidance Agreements (DTAs) prevent double taxation on foreign dividends, interest, and capital gains.
        </li>
